{
  "narrative": "Optimised prompt templates markedly improve LLM screening performance, with Cao et al. reporting 97.7% sensitivity and 85.2% specificity for abstract screening using GPT‑4‑0125‑preview, far exceeding zero‑shot prompts (49% sensitivity) [R0006]. Oami et al. observed a rise in sensitivity from 0.75 to 0.91 after post‑hoc prompt modifications while maintaining high specificity (0.98) for GPT‑4 Turbo, indicating that prompt refinement can boost recall without sacrificing precision [R0028]. Homiar et al. achieved 100% sensitivity for ultimately included studies with GPT‑4o after iterative prompt development, though precision remained low (4–20%), reflecting a trade‑off between recall and false‑positive burden [R0089]. Qiu et al. demonstrated consistent high sensitivities (98–100%) across three datasets using a structured, stepwise prompting workflow with ChatGPT, with specificities ranging from 80.6% to 93% and a need for human oversight of residual false positives at the full‑text stage [R0064]. Sanghera et al. showed that prompt choice strongly influences performance: on a balanced 800‑record set, optimal prompts yielded perfect sensitivity (1.00) and high precision (0.927), whereas less tailored prompts produced variable sensitivity (0.756–1.00) and markedly lower precision (0.004–0.096) on a large 119 695‑record dataset [R0057]. Across studies, higher sensitivity is consistently achieved with carefully engineered prompts, but specificity and precision vary, likely due to differences in LLM versions, dataset composition, and the extent of prompt optimisation. Evidence is limited by reliance on abstracts only in some reports and by some‑concern risk of bias assessments.",
