{
  "narrative": "Direct comparisons of LLMs show heterogeneous accuracy for title/abstract screening. Insuk et al. reported human reviewers highest accuracy (0.9593) followed by Claude (0.9448) and ChatGPT (0.9138) [R0039]; however this study provides only abstract data and has unclear risk of bias. Oami et al. found GPT-4o achieved high specificity (0.98) but lower sensitivity (0.75), whereas Gemini 1.5 Pro and Claude 3.5 Sonnet showed higher sensitivities (0.93 and 0.95) but lower specificities (0.86 and 0.81) [R0040]. Sood et al. observed Gemini Flash 1.5 highest overall accuracy (96.02%) and GPT‑4 the highest sensitivity (95.97%) [R0052]. Fazeli et al. reported GPT‑4o mini accuracy of 85.1% with sensitivity 83.2% and specificity 85.2% on a large record set [R0053]. Zhan et al. found Review Copilot (based on GPT‑4) achieving the highest sensitivity (99.2%) but moderate specificity (83.6%) [R0070]. Li et al. showed ChatGPT v4.0 reaching sensitivity 0.93, specificity 0.96 and accuracy 0.945 on a benchmark dataset [R0074]. Kwok et al. noted commercial LLMs (ChatGPT, Copilot) with accuracy 83‑86% and recall ~88% in physiotherapy trials [R0048]. Across studies, models trade off sensitivity versus specificity, and performance varies with prompting, dataset, and evaluation design. Studies with larger sample sizes (e.g., Fazeli, n=15,605) tend to report lower accuracy than smaller or abstract‑only reports, suggesting possible over‑estimation in limited data. Overall, no single model consistently outperforms others across all metrics.",
