{
  "narrative": "Across five investigations, LLMs consistently reduced screening workload compared with human reviewers. Oami et al. reported that processing 100 studies fell from 17.2 minutes to 1.3 minutes, a mean saving of 15.25 minutes per 100 records [R0028]. Kwok et al. found that commercial LLMs cut total screening time to 37% of that required by humans, equating to roughly a three‑fold speedup [R0048]. Li et al. observed that their LLM tools required only 10–20 minutes to process 200 abstracts, indicating high time efficiency relative to manual duplicate screening [R0074]. Qiu et al. demonstrated that a structured prompt‑based workflow enabled screening of 3,821 abstracts and 261 full texts without programming, though human oversight remained necessary for false‑positive correction [R0064]. Zhan et al. reported that Review Copilot completed title/abstract screening in one‑quarter of human time and could exclude 90–95% of irrelevant articles at a rate of 1,000 articles per four person‑hours [R0070]. While all studies show substantial time savings, the magnitude varies with LLM version, prompting strategy, and dataset size, and some reports rely on abstracts only or have limited sample description, introducing uncertainty about generalisability.",
  "certainty": "moderate",
  "certainty_rationale": "Evidence is downgraded for risk of bias (unblinded designs, limited reporting) and inconsistency in reporting metrics and sample sizes, but remains moderate due to consistent direction of effect across multiple studies."
}

{
  "narrative": "A comparative evaluation using six open‑source SYNERGY datasets examined the performance of a zero‑shot Llama‑3.1‑8B‑Instruct large language model (LLM) against four traditional supervised machine‑learning (SML) classifiers for title‑abstract screening. The LLM achieved a recall of 0.86, which was comparable to or higher than the recall reported for human reviewers, whereas the SML models (logistic regression, random forest, support vector machine) showed higher specificity than humans but lower recall [R0042]. Specificity for the LLM was 0.79, falling between the higher specificity of the SML models and the lower specificity of human screening. Performance varied markedly across the six datasets, indicating that neither approach consistently outperformed the other in all contexts [R0042]. The authors note that the comparison is limited to TF‑IDF‑based SML baselines and a zero‑shot, criteria‑prompted LLM, and that dataset selection bias and the subjectivity of human‑assigned labels may affect generalisability [R0042]. No pooled metric is presented because of the heterogeneity of results across datasets.",
  "certainty": "very low",
  "certainty_rationale": "Observational design with some concerns of bias, high inconsistency across datasets, and indirectness due to limited model types and dataset selection."
}

{
  "summary_of_evidence": "The 13 included studies show that large language models (LLMs) can achieve very high sensitivity for title and abstract screening when prompts are carefully engineered, with reported sensitivities ranging from 0.75 to 100 % and specificities from 0.80 to 0.98 [R0006,R0028,R0089,R0064,R0057]. Optimised prompt templates consistently improve recall, although precision often declines, reflecting a trade‑off between missed studies and false‑positive burden. Direct model comparisons reveal heterogeneous performance: some models (e.g., Claude, Gemini Flash) attain higher overall accuracy, while others (e.g., GPT‑4, Review Copilot) achieve the highest sensitivity but lower specificity [R0039,R0040,R0052,R0053,R0070,R0074,R0048]. Larger datasets tend to yield modest accuracy (≈85‑86 %) compared with smaller, abstract‑only samples that report higher metrics, suggesting possible over‑estimation in limited data. Across five investigations, LLMs markedly reduce screening time, cutting workload to 25‑40 % of that required by humans and saving 10‑15 minutes per 100 records screened [R0028,R0048,R0074,R0064,R0070]. A single zero‑shot LLM (Llama‑3.1‑8B‑Instruct) demonstrated recall comparable to humans but lower specificity than traditional supervised‑machine‑learning classifiers, indicating that LLMs and SML approaches each have strengths and weaknesses [R0042]. Overall, evidence is low to moderate certainty due to risk‑of‑bias concerns and variability in datasets and prompting strategies.",
  "limitations_of_evidence": "All studies carry at least some concerns for bias, often stemming from unblinded designs, lack of independent validation sets, and reliance on abstract‑only data in five reports, which limits assessment of full‑text relevance [R0006,R0028,R0089,R0064,R0057]. Reported performance metrics are inconsistent across models and prompting methods, and many studies omit confidence intervals or detailed sample size calculations, reducing precision. The heterogeneity of datasets (size, topic, balance) and the use of different LLM versions impede direct comparability and introduce indirectness. Moreover, few investigations evaluated long‑term workflow integration or the impact of false‑positive burden on downstream screening stages.",
  "implications": "Practitioners considering LLMs for systematic review screening should prioritize prompt optimisation to maximise sensitivity, while planning for human oversight to manage false positives. The demonstrated time savings suggest LLMs can accelerate the screening phase, but variability in specificity warrants caution, especially for large, unbalanced corpora. Future research should standardise evaluation protocols, include full‑text screening outcomes, and report uncertainty measures. Comparative trials of multiple LLMs and supervised‑machine‑learning classifiers on diverse, well‑characterised datasets are needed to identify conditions under which each approach offers the greatest efficiency and accuracy."
}

